1. d4 Nf6 2. Nc3 d5 3. Bf4 e6 {This is the most popular move on Lichess, and it is the one you are likely to face at least half the time. Black opens up the dark-squared bishop and establishes a firmer presence in the center.} 4. Nb5 {This is a very important move that forces Black to make a positional concession. Black must either give away the bishop pair and damage his pawn structure with 4...Bd6, or permanently misplace his knight with 4...Na6. In the latter case, Black will push away the knight with ...c6, but the knight from a6 will still have a very hard time rejoining the game.} Bd6 {There is no question that this is the worst of Black's three main lines, but it is the most popular by a country mile.} (4... Na6 {This is the most straightforward response. Black defends the c7-pawn without giving away the bishop pair. In this line, the strategic potency of Nb5 is on full display: the knight on a6 will have a very difficult time rejoining society: the shortest path involves ferrying the knight around to d6 via c7 and e8, which already takes a staggering number of moves. Our general approach is to quickly complete our kingside development. After we do that...okay, how about we cross that bridge when we get there!} 5. e3 {Black is at a further crossroads. He can chase the knight away immediately with 5...c6, focus on kingside development with 5...Be7, or strike at the center straightaway with 5...c5. All of these eventualities must be carefully considered - each leads to a fundamentally different type of battle.} Be7 {The main move at a high level. Black retains maximum flexibility, developing his kingside and reserving the possibility to chase away White's knight at a more convenient moment.} (5... c6 6. Nc3 {It is quite clear that our journey to b5 has been anything but a waste of time. Not only is Black's knight awkwardly placed, but it is outright vulnerable: Black must now reckon with the positional threat of Bxa6, permanently ruining Black's queenside structure and creating a massive hole on c5 that we can easily access with our knight.} Bd6 (6... Bb4 7. Bxa6 {We certainly don't miss the opportunity to create massive, long-term weaknesses on the queenside.} bxa6 8. Nf3 {Calmly continuing our development. It is important to remember that the combination of ...Bb4 and ...Ne4 is almost never scary in the Jobava: we almost always respond by castling, giving up the c3-pawn but attaining a massive initiative in return. In this case, we will also attain total positional domination on the queenside.} O-O (8... Qa5 9. O-O {As with 8...Ne4, we are more than happy to give up a pawn in exchange for full control over the dark squares.} Bxc3 10. bxc3 Qxc3 11. Rb1 $1 {Seizing the b-file and preventing Black from deploying the bishop to b7.} O-O 12. Bd6 {Striking while the iron is hot!} Re8 13. Bb4 Qc4 {The queen has been forced onto a more vulnerable square, enabling our knight to jump into the action.} 14. Ne5 Qb5 15. c4 $1 {Now, another pawn is temporarily sacrificed in order to tear open the queenside and increase the power of our initiative.} dxc4 16. Bc5 Qa5 17. Qf3 $18 {Black is absolutely screwed: he can barely move, his queenside is collapsing, and our pieces are on optimal squares. This line really showcases the multi-faceted nature of the Jobava London: it is an opening that promises its adherents not just tactical victories, but strategic masterpieces as well!}) (8... Ne4 9. O-O Nxc3 10. bxc3 Bxc3 11. Rb1 {In contrast to the 8...Qa5 line, here Black keeps the dark-squared bishop, but it is unable to move back to the a3-f8 diagonal. As such, White still holds complete domination over the dark squares, and the imminent prospect of Ne5 will push Black over the edge.} O-O (11... f6 {Preventing Ne5 does more harm than good:} 12. Qd3 Ba5 13. Qa3 {Preventing Black from castling, and reinforcing our control over key dark squares along the a3-f8 diagonal.} Bc7 (13... Bb6 14. c4 $1 {We saw a similar move in the 8...Qa5 line (see White's 15th move). If the pawn is captured, Black's entire queenside will be in tatters; if it isn't, the pawn will deliver a further blow to Black's defensive capacity once it reaches c5.} Kf7 15. c5 Bc7 16. Bd6 $1 {And now, we entrench the bishop on the sexiest outpost on the board!} Re8 17. e4 $1 {A lovely positional sacrifice, giving up the center pawn in order to carve out a devastating path for our knight.} dxe4 18. Nd2 f5 19. Nc4 Kg8 20. Ne5 {It took us a while, but we finally got our knight on a perfect square! c6 is hanging, and Black has no choice but to allow the infiltration of our rook.} Bd7 21. Rb7 $18 {This line is worth reviewing several times. Note that White sacrifices three pawns, and all three sacrifices are entirely positional in nature. First, the c3-pawn is sacrificed to spark the flames of the initiative. Then, after the queen is ferried to a3, we open up the queenside with c2-c4. Once Black declines the pawn, it is driven to c5, and we finish with a sacrifice that enables us to carve out a devastating pathway for our knight. A certified Jobava Classic!}) 14. Bxc7 Qxc7 15. c4 Qe7 16. Qa4 $16) 12. Ne5 Bd7 (12... Qe8 {Defending the pawn with the queen prevents Rb7, but opens Black up to a devastating attack on the kingside.} 13. Rb3 {It is worth flicking in this move, which forces Black's bishop on an even more awkward square.} Ba5 14. Qg4 {Suddenly, we transition from the queenside to the kingside! It turns out that our previous move had a hidden aim: White now intends the gorgeous e3-e4, opening up a pathway from the queenside to the kingside for our rook! In addition, we threaten the banal Bh6, forcing Black to burn a crucial tempo.} Kh8 (14... f6 15. Bh6 $18) 15. e4 $1 {There it is! We saw this same move in the 11...f6 sub-variation, although it was played for an entirely different reason (to get the knight to e5). Here, the knight is already on e5, and this move is played in order to lift the rook over to the kingside.} dxe4 (15... f5 16. Qh4 Bd8 17. Bg5 Bxg5 18. Qxg5 {Note that total dominance of our knight over Black's entire army!} fxe4 19. Rg3 Rg8 {The king is now boxed in, and a beautiful mating pattern seals the deal:} (19... g6 20. h4 Rf5 21. Nxg6+ Qxg6 22. Qd8+ $18) 20. Ng6+ Qxg6 21. Qxg6 hxg6 22. Rh3#) 16. Rg3 g6 17. Qh4 $18 {with endless threats against Black's king, all of them unstoppable.}) 13. Rb7 Be8 14. Qg4 $18 {We could keep going, but we think you'll be able to take it from here! White dominates on both sides of the board.}) (8... a5 {A sensible move, preparing to deploy the light-squared bishop on a6. Still, it does not address the gross structural problems on the queenside, and our response is rather straightforward.} 9. O-O {Obviously, it is important to castle before Black brings the bishop to a6.} Ba6 (9... Bxc3 10. bxc3 Ba6 11. Re1 Ne4 {Naroditsky-Makhnyov, Chess.com (blitz) 2023. Now, the natural response is 12.Ne5, but it is beat out by a scintillating positional sacrifice:} 12. c4 $1 Bxc4 (12... O-O 13. c5 f6 14. Nd2 Nc3 15. Qg4 Re8 16. a4) 13. Ne5 O-O (13... Bb5 14. c4 dxc4 (14... Bxc4 15. Rc1 O-O 16. Nxc4 dxc4 17. Rxc4 Qd5 18. Qc2 Rfc8 19. Rc1 $16) 15. a4 Ba6 16. Qf3 Qd5 17. Bh6 $3 gxh6 (17... O-O 18. Qg4 g6 19. f3 Nd6 20. Nd7 $18 {[%cal Rd7f6,Rd7f8]}) 18. Qxf7+ Kd8 19. f3 Ng5 20. Qf6+ Kc7 21. Qg7+ Kb6 22. Nd7+ Kc7 23. Nf6+ $18) 14. f3 Nd6 (14... Nf6 15. e4 $14) 15. Nxc6 Qc7 16. Bxd6 Qxd6 17. Nxa5 Rfc8 18. Nb7 Qb4 19. Nc5 Qc3 20. Qc1 Rab8 21. Rd1 $14 {[%cal Rc1d2]}) 10. Re1 O-O (10... Ne4 {As usual, this move is futile, but this time for a more tactical reason:} 11. Nxe4 {Exploiting the uncastled nature of Black's king!} Bxe1 (11... dxe4 12. Nd2 O-O 13. c3 Be7 14. Nxe4 $18) 12. Nd6+ $18 {We pick up the bishop on the next move, with a decisive material advantage.}) 11. Ne5 Rc8 12. Bg5 $1 {An important idea, that re-occurs several times throughout this course. While this pin may appear empty, it in fact causes Black very serious headaches. We threaten to capture the knight on f6 and follow up with a fork on d7, an idea that is often overlooked even by extremely strong grandmasters. If Black drops the bishop back to e7, then the c3-knight is liberated, and we can establish our control over the c5-square with Na4.} h6 13. Bxf6 gxf6 {A sad necessity, but this creates long-term weaknesses on the kingside.} (13... Qxf6 14. Nd7 $18) 14. Nd3 Bd6 15. e4 $14) 9. O-O a5 (9... Bxc3 $6 10. bxc3 Ne4 11. c4 dxc4 12. Ne5 Qd5 13. f3 Nf6 14. e4 $18) (9... c5 10. Na4 cxd4 (10... c4 11. c3 Be7 12. b3 cxb3 (12... Nh5 13. Bg3 Nxg3 14. hxg3 Qc7 15. Qc2 Bd7 16. Nb2 cxb3 (16... Bb5 17. bxc4 Bxc4 18. Nxc4 Qxc4 19. Nd2 Qc7 20. c4 dxc4 21. Qxc4 Qxc4 22. Nxc4 $14) 17. axb3 Rab8 18. Ne5 Be8 19. Ned3 a5 20. c4 $14) 13. axb3 Nh5 14. Bg3 Nxg3 15. hxg3 Qc7 16. Qc2 $16 {[%cal Rc3c4]}) 11. exd4 Bd7 12. c3 Be7 13. Nc5 $14 Bxc5 14. dxc5 Ne4 15. b4 Nxc3 16. Qd3 Ne4 17. Qxa6 Qc8 18. Qa5 $14) 10. Ne5 c5 11. Re1 Ba6 12. Bg5 $1 {We saw a very similar idea in the 8...a5 variation, with the minor difference that Black's rook was on a8 and the pawn was still on c6. These cosmetic differences change very little: White still threatens Bxf6 followed by Nd7, and White is still very much incapable of dealing with the problems that await him on both sides of the board.} cxd4 (12... h6 13. Nc6 {A pretty in-betweener, driving the queen away from d8 and setting up Bxf6.} Qd7 14. Bxf6 gxf6 (14... Qxc6 15. Qg4 g6 16. Qh4 Kh7 17. a3 Bxc3 18. bxc3 $40) 15. Nxb4 cxb4 16. Nb1 Kh7 17. c3 $14 {White is better on both sides of the board!}) 13. exd4 Be7 {Instead of the regular 14.Na4, here we have a tactic that leads to immediate victory:} 14. Nc6 Qd7 15. Nxe7+ Qxe7 16. Nxd5 $18) (6... Nc7 {With this move, Black takes away the possibility of Bxa6, but the knight is hardly in a better place on c7. We follow up in typical fashion, developing our knight to f3 and bishop to d3.} 7. Nf3 Bb4 (7... Bd6 8. Ne5 {See typical idea #1: the knight is once again placed between the two bishops!} O-O (8... Qe7 9. Qf3 {Yet another textbook move, preparing to lift the queen up to h3 in the event that Black castles. If Black tries to root out the knight on e5 straightaway, a secondary point of this move is revealed:} Nd7 10. Nxf7 $1 {Boom shaka-laka! The queen on f3 renders ...Kxf7 impossible, and Black has no choice but to part ways with a very important pawn.} Bxf4 11. Qxf4 {An important detail: the myopic 11.Nxh8 led to a far more double-edged position, since we would run the risk of losing the knight.} Qxf7 12. Qxc7 O-O 13. f4 {Defending the pawn and preventing ...e5!} Qe8 14. O-O-O Rf7 15. Qd6 $18) 9. Bd3 {We have completed our minor piece development, and are fully ready to initiate aggressive action on the kingside. We will do so by flying our queen to h3, and, if necessary, supplementing with g4-g5. Black's main issue is that he has nothing going on the queenside, giving us a free hand on the other side of the board. The power of the knight on e5 can hardly be overstated: it lords over the entire position, making it virtually impossible for Black to get anything going in the way of counterplay.} c5 {The most logical move: Black initiates counterplay in the center and aims to undermine our knight. As it turns out, we are more than happy to go along!} (9... Qe7 {A sensible attempt to prepare ...Nd7 by protecting the bishop. However, it is much to slow, and the knight on e5 is so well-defended that Black will not possibly be able to get it to move!} 10. Qf3 Nd7 {Here, Black puts more immediate pressure on the knight, so it is worth amending the queen's path to lend the knight further support:} 11. Qg3 Nxe5 (11... Bxe5 12. dxe5 $16) (11... Ne8 {Now, we engineer a curious queen shuffle to provoke even more weaknesses in Black's kingside.} 12. Qh3 g6 13. Qg3 {Black is virtually paralyzed, and any attempt to free himself leads to immediate calamity:} f6 14. Bxg6 $1 {This was the reason for inducing ...g6!} hxg6 15. Nxg6 Qg7 16. Bxd6 Nxd6 17. Ne7+ $1 Kf7 18. Qxd6 $18 {Picking off the knight and defending our own! Of course, the knight on e7 has a very convenient escape hatch through c8! The capture on g2 is utterly innocuous and can be met with 0-0-0.}) 12. dxe5 Bb4 13. Bg5 Qe8 (13... Qd7 14. Qh4 Bxc3+ 15. bxc3 h6 16. g4 $1 Ne8 17. Bxh6 gxh6 18. g5 $18) 14. Bf6 $18) (9... Nd7 $2 {This loses immediately to a familiar tactic:} 10. Nxf7 Rxf7 11. Bxd6 Ne8 12. Bg3 $18) 10. dxc5 {The knight on e5 no longer requires the support of the d4-pawn, since the bishop does the job perfectly well.} Bxc5 11. Qf3 {Now, it is time for typical idea #5: the queen is ferried over to h3 before initiating a furious kingside storm.} Bd6 (11... d4 12. exd4 Bxd4 13. O-O-O $1 Ncd5 14. Bxh7+ Kxh7 15. Rxd4 $18) 12. Qh3 g6 13. g4 $40 {Having induced serious weaknesses on the kingside, we move on to the pawn storm phase. Our next step is to shift the queen to g3 in order to leave open the path for the h-pawn.} Nce8 (13... Nd7 14. Nxg6 $1 fxg6 15. Bxd6 $18) 14. Qg3 Qc7 15. O-O-O {It is always important to be patient in the Jobava London, and this line is no exception. Before rolling up our sleeves on the kingside, we ensure the safety of our king (and threaten Nb5).} a6 16. h4 Nd7 17. Nxd7 Bxd7 18. Bxd6 Nxd6 19. h5 $40 {While the computer will likely hold off the attack, we believe that the position is totally untenable from a human perspective. We will either open the h-file with hxg6 or, in the event of ...g5, smash open the kingside with f2-f4.}) 8. Bd3 Nb5 9. O-O {As always, we are more than willing to part ways with a pawn in order to achieve a harmonious setup and gain control of key queenside squares (typical idea #8).} Nxc3 10. bxc3 Bxc3 11. Rb1 O-O 12. Bg5 {A familiar idea, establishing a very nasty pin against Black's knight.} h6 13. Bh4 $44 {White's compensation is absolutely massive: Black has no good way to end the pin, since ...g5 creates fatal weaknesses on the kingside and ...Qc7 allows us to capture on f6. We can follow up by intensifying the pressure with Ne5 and f2-f4, followed by a rook or queen lift to the kingside. On the queenside, Black has no active prospects and no good way to activate the light-squared bishop.}) (6... Nb8 {This knight transfer is incredibly inefficient. Black will eventually re-route the knight to a relatively active square, but the tempi that he burns in the process will grant us an excellent game.} 7. Nf3 Bb4 8. Bd3 c5 9. dxc5 Nbd7 10. O-O Nxc5 (10... Bxc3 11. bxc3 Nxc5 12. Ne5 O-O 13. Bg5 $14) 11. Nb5 {Black has spent endless tempi to get the knight to an active square, only to be sent back to a6!} Na6 (11... O-O 12. Nc7 Rb8 13. Nxd5 $1 $18 {Winning material.})) 7. Bxa6 {Typical idea #4: we do not miss an opportunity to cripple Black's queenside pawn structure and create a massive hole on c5.} bxa6 (7... Bxf4 8. Bxb7 {A nice desperado move to pick up a pawn.} Bxb7 9. exf4 $14) 8. Nf3 c5 {Pushing the pawn may eradicate the weakness on c6, but it does not rid us of the outpost on c5.} (8... Bxf4 9. exf4 {We are almost always happy to achieve the d4-f4 pawn bind, which resembles a Maroczy Bind shifted one file to the right.} O-O 10. Na4 Ne4 {The knight on e4 appears imposing, but it can be kicked away at our convenience with f2-f3.} (10... Qa5+ 11. c3 $16) (10... Nd7 11. O-O Qc7 12. Ne5 f6 13. Nxd7 Bxd7 14. Nc5 Rae8 15. Qg4 $16) 11. O-O Qd6 12. Ne5 f6 13. Nd3 $16 {The knight has been ferried over to an ideal square. Now, both knights control c5, and we threaten to win the knight with f2-f3. The position plays itself: Nac5 can be followed by Re1, when Black is sinking under the weight of his endless weaknesses.}) (8... Rb8 {Attacking b2 does not change our plans in the slightest, since we were planning to play Na4 anyway.} 9. Na4 Bxf4 10. exf4 c5 11. Nxc5 Rxb2 12. O-O $16) 9. Bxd6 Qxd6 10. Na4 cxd4 11. exd4 Ne4 12. Ne5 O-O (12... f6 $2 13. Qh5+ g6 14. Nxg6 $18) 13. f3 Bd7 14. c3 Nf6 15. Nc5 $16) (5... c5 $6 {As usual, this move makes very little sense, since it enables the knight to remain on b5 forever.} 6. c3 Be7 7. a4 {It is always a good idea to reinforce the knight, taking the sting out of ...Bd7.} Bd7 8. Nf3 O-O 9. Bd3 Nh5 10. Be5 f6 {Now, rather than the meek 11.g3, we can exploit the vulnerability of Black's knight with a lovely tactic:} 11. Ng5 $1 g6 12. Nxh7 $1 {Now, 12...Kxh7 leads to rapid checkmate after 13.Qxh5, so Black must part ways with a lot of material and give up the final pawn which provides shelter to the king.} fxe5 13. Nxf8 Qxf8 14. Bxg6 Ng7 15. Qg4 Be8 16. dxe5 $18 {with three pawns and a rook for two pieces, as well as domination on tthe queenside and the straightforward idea of pushing the h-pawn all the way to h6.}) (5... Bb4+ {An interesting move, inducing c2-c3 in order to take away our knight's typical retreating square. However, we are able to find alternate arrangements for our knight just in time.} 6. c3 Be7 (6... Ba5 7. Qa4 $18) 7. a4 {This is not played to reinforce the knight, so much as to establish contact between the rook and the a3-square, rendering ...Bxa3 ineffective once the knight retreats.} O-O 8. Bd3 c6 9. Na3 c5 10. Nb5 {Right back to home base!} Bd7 11. Nf3 c4 12. Bb1 {We have achieved our ideal minor piece setup, and can improve our position in a litany of ways.} Qb6 (12... Ne4 13. Bxe4 dxe4 14. Nd2 $16) (12... Bxb5 13. axb5 Nc7 14. Qc2 Nxb5 15. Ne5 Nd6 (15... g6 16. h4 Nh5 17. Bh6 Re8 18. g4 Nf6 19. f3 $40) 16. f3) 13. Qc2 {Needless to say, we could not care less about giving up a measly pawn on the queenside. With this move, we set up a queen-bishop battery, which will likely lead to a swift and decisive attack against Black's king.} Bxb5 14. axb5 Qxb5 15. Ng5 g6 16. h4 {Typical idea #3: we rush the h-pawn up the board, and Black has too many weaknesses and not enough defenders on the kingside to do anything about it.} Qb3 17. Qe2 $1 {Obviously, we do not even think about trading queens!} h6 18. Nf3 Kg7 19. h5 g5 20. Nxg5 {A thematic piece sacrifice, smashing open the kingside. With the queen entirely out of commission on b3, Black cannot lend nearly enough reinforcement to the kingside and the attack will end the game in a matter of a few moves.} hxg5 21. h6+ Kh8 22. Be5 Nb8 23. Ra3 Qb6 24. Bxf6+ Bxf6 25. Qc2 $18 {Rebuilding the battery! This time, Black has no pawn to put on g6, and the bishop blocks the f-pawn. Checkmate is coming on the next move.}) 6. Nf3 O-O 7. h4 $5 {A very interesting move favored by GM Arjun Erigaisi, one of the leading specialists of this line. White's idea could not possibly be more straightforward: we want to checkmate Black on the kingside! As a bonus, this move opens up an escape square for the bishop on h2, which takes the sting out of a knight jump to h5. The engine is slow to approve of this move, but careful analysis shows that Black's position is incredibly difficult to handle.} c6 (7... c5 8. c3 Bd7 9. a4 {As always, this kind of position favors White, since the knight on b5 cannot be easily driven away.} c4 (9... Ne4 10. Bd3 c4 11. Bxe4 dxe4 12. Nd2 $16 {Picking up a pawn.}) 10. b3 {Chipping away at Black's pawn chain. Now, 10...cxb3 enables us to develop our bishop to an ideal square, while ...Rc8 drops the pawn on a7. Black has only one other way to try to maintain the pawn on c4, but it fails as well.} Qa5 {Hoping to meet bxc4 with ...Bxb5 followed by ...Qxc3+. But we have a lovely positional move up our sleeve:} (10... cxb3 11. Bd3 b2 12. Rb1 Ne4 13. Rxb2 $16) 11. Nd2 $146 {Gorgeous. By covering the e1-a5 diagonal, we ensure that ...Qxc3 does not come with check, so 11...Bxb5 no longer works. After the trade on b3 (which is forced, since we threaten to take on c4), the queen will be driven back to b6 and we will finally be able to deploy our light-squared bishop to d3.} cxb3 12. Nxb3 Qb6 (12... Qd8 13. Bd3 $14) 13. Nd2 {It is a good idea to shuttle our knight back to d2 in order to reinforce the e4-square and open up the b-file.} Rfc8 14. Bd3 Bxb5 15. axb5 Rxc3 {It appears that Black has gotten the better of us tactically, but yet another scintillating retreating knight move proves otherwise.} 16. Nb1 $3 {Simply divine! The knight moves back to its initial square, establishing contact between the queen and bishop, and facing Black with two threats at the same time. Black has nothing better than to give up the exchange, when his compensation will be woefully insufficient.} Rxd3 17. Qxd3 Nb4 18. Qe2 Rc8 19. O-O {Our king has been ferried to safety, and Black has nothing going on on the queenside.} Rc2 20. Qd1 $16) (7... Ne4 {This move looks like a sensible way to take away the c3-square, but in fact it takes away nothing, since Nc3 is still very much possible; the subsequent damage that we suffer on the queenside is nothing more than cosmetic. Furthermore, the knight on f6 was integral to the long-term safety of the king, and with the knight gone, we have every chance to develop a quick and decisive kingside attack.} 8. Bd3 c6 9. Nc3 Bb4 10. O-O Nxc3 11. bxc3 Bxc3 {Should we meekly shift the rook to b1? Absolutely not! With the knight no longer on f6, the correct idea should be second nature to a seasoned tactician.} 12. Bxh7+ {Greek gift time! Yes, seemingly every idea has its moment in the sun in the Jobava London. This move decides the game instantly.} Kxh7 13. Ng5+ Kg8 14. Qh5 Re8 15. Qxf7+ {A classic concept, removing the pawn from f7 before returning to h5 and delivering check on h7. Black is mated by force.} Kh8 16. Qh5+ Kg8 17. Qh7+ Kf8 18. Qh8+ Ke7 19. Qxg7#) 8. Nc3 c5 (8... Qa5 9. Be2 Ne4 10. O-O Nxc3 11. bxc3 Qxc3 {Yet again, we are happy to part ways with a pawn (typical idea #8).} 12. h5 h6 (12... c5 13. h6 g6 14. Rb1 c4 15. e4 dxe4 16. Ne5 $16) 13. Bxa6 bxa6 14. Rb1 $16 {[%csl Rc8] We dominate on both sides of the board, and can follow up with Ne5.}) (8... Nc7 9. Bd3 Nh5 $4 (9... h6 10. Ne5 $18 {g4-g5 will be utterly crushing.}) (9... c5 10. dxc5 Bxc5 11. g4 $1 Nxg4 (11... Bb4 12. g5 Ne4 13. Bxe4 dxe4 14. Qxd8 Rxd8 15. Bxc7 Rd7 16. Ne5 $1 Rxc7 17. O-O-O Be7 18. Nxe4 $16) 12. Bxh7+ {This isn't a greek gift sacrifice, but rather an ingenious way to weaken the king permanently and win back the pawn.} Kxh7 13. Ng5+ Kg8 14. Qxg4 e5 15. Qh5 Bf5 16. Bxe5 f6 17. Bxc7 Qxc7 18. Nf3 g6 19. Rg1 Qg7 {It appears that our queen is trapped, but we have a nice way to trade it off for its counterpart.} 20. Qxf5 gxf5 21. Rxg7+ Kxg7 22. Nxd5 $14 {We have two pawns for the exchange, and Black's incredibly weak kingside pawns will give us plenty of targets. Furthermore, the h4-pawn - once an attacker - has now become a fearsome passed pawn which will keep Black's rooks at bay.}) 10. Bxh7+ Kxh7 11. Ng5+ Kg6 12. Be5 {Black's king is caught in the crossfire of a lovely attack that quickly results in mate.} f6 13. Qd3+ f5 14. g4 Nf6 15. h5+ Kxg5 16. Bf4+ Kxg4 17. Qe2#) 9. h5 {Driving the pawn down to h5 and creating the rather nasty threat of h5-h6, which will provoke long-term weaknesses on the kingside.} h6 (9... Bd7 10. h6 g6 11. Bxa6 bxa6 12. dxc5 Bxc5 13. Bg5 Be7 14. Qd4 $18 {Dark-square domination! Black is utterly paralyzed.}) 10. g4 {Time to rip open the g-file! White's attack proceeds at a furious pace, while Black has very little going on the other side of the board.} Nxg4 (10... cxd4 11. exd4 Ne4 (11... Nxg4 12. Rg1 f5 13. Qd2 Bd7 14. O-O-O $40) 12. Bd3 Nxc3 13. bxc3 f6 (13... Bd7 14. Rb1 Qc8 15. g5 $1 hxg5 16. Bxg5 Bxg5 17. Nxg5 Qxc3+ 18. Kf1 $18) 14. Qd2 e5 15. dxe5 Bxg4 16. Rg1 $1 f5 (16... Bxf3 17. Bxh6 g5 $8 (17... Rf7 18. e6 $18) 18. Qe3 Bxh5 19. Qh3 Qe8 20. Bxf8 Kxf8 (20... Bxf8 21. Qf5 Qf7 22. exf6 $18) 21. Rh1 $18) 17. Nd4 $18) 11. Rg1 f5 12. Ne5 {The g4-knight is the last obstacle standing between White and Black's monarch. Note that we are not just playing on the kingside: the long-term threat of a well-timed capture on a6 is yet another headache that Black must manage.} Nxe5 13. Bxe5 Bf6 14. Bxf6 Qxf6 15. Bxa6 {It is the perfect opportunity to capture on a6, since we will follow up by capturing on c5 and opening up the d4-square for our queen.} bxa6 (15... cxd4 16. Rg6 $1 Qe7 (16... Qh4 17. Qxd4 Qxd4 18. exd4 bxa6 19. Kd2 $16) 17. Qxd4 bxa6 18. O-O-O $16) 16. dxc5 Bd7 17. Qd4 Be8 18. Ne2 Bxh5 19. Nf4 Bg4 20. Nd3 $16 {[%csl Re5] Despite the loss of a pawn, White maintains complete dominance of the key e5-square, which will be used as an outpost for the knight. This, coupled with the passed pawn on c5, gives White overwhelming compensation for the pawn.}) (4... Bb4+ {An ingenious way to defend the c7-pawn: the bishop travels back to a5. This is the least popular of Black's three alternatives, yet it is the choice of many top-level players and, in our opinion, constitutes Black's best objective challenge to 4.Nb5. We spent countless hours on this move, and came up with several fascinating ideas that confront Black with serious challenges. The resulting positions are convoluted, so we will try to carefully explain White's main ideas with text and illustrative variations.} 5. c3 {The first step is obvious: we block the check with our pawn, sending the bishop back to a5.} Ba5 6. a4 $1 {A clever, dastardly resource! Rather than putting our cards on the table with the immediate 6.b4, we set up the threat of b2-b4 and a4-a5, which will trap the bishop on b6. Black must respond by attempting to chase away our knight.} a6 {A critical position. It may appear that White's queenside play has ground to a complete halt, but the party is only beginning.} (6... c6 {This is a more desirable way to chase away the knight from a strategic point of view, but it contains a fatal tactical flaw: Black grants us access to the d6-square!}) 7. b4 $1 {Counterattacking the bishop! This is the first of many fantastic tactical moves that pose serious problems for Black on the queenside.} axb5 {The most popular move.} (7... Bb6 8. a5 axb5 (8... Bxa5 9. Rxa5 $18) 9. axb6 Rxa1 (9... Na6 10. e3 c6 11. Bd3 Qxb6 12. Nf3 $44 {White has overwhelming compensation for the pawn.}) 10. Qxa1 Na6 (10... cxb6 $4 {Believe it or not, over a thousand people on Lichess made this terrible blunder!} 11. Bxb8 $18) 11. bxc7 Nxc7 12. Qa5 $16) (7... Bxb4 {A tricky desperado sacrifice: it is crucial to refrain from 8.Nxc7+ due to 8...Qxc7, and Black regains the queen with an extra piece.} 8. cxb4 axb5 9. axb5 Rxa1 10. Qxa1 {White has three main advantages: control over the a-file, the bishop pair, and, most importantly, the doubled pawns, which in this position are a major asset. The pawn on b5 fully restricts Black's knight, and threatens to advance to b6. From a practical standpoint, there is no question that White's game is much easier.} O-O (10... Qe7 11. Qa3 {We defend the pawn while maintaining control over the a-file. In addition, 11.Qc3 would run into 11...Ne4.} Nh5 12. Bg3 Nxg3 13. hxg3 O-O 14. e3 {White's position is preferrable:we have quite a bit more space, long-term pressure down the h-file, as well as total domination over key queenside squares and the eventual possibility of pressure down the c-file.}) (10... Nh5 11. Be5 Nd7 (11... O-O 12. Qc1 $1 $14 {[%csl Rc7][%cal Re5c7,Rc1c7]}) (11... f6 12. Bg3 Nd7 13. e3) 12. h4 $1 {An incredible move, expanding on the kingside and threatening the devastating g4. In addition, White creates an escape square for the dark-squared bishop!} f6 (12... Nxe5 13. dxe5 O-O (13... d4 14. Nf3 $14) 14. e3 g6 (14... d4 15. Qxd4 Qxd4 16. exd4 Rd8 17. Ne2 $16) 15. Nf3 $16) (12... O-O 13. e3 Nxe5 14. dxe5 g6 15. Nf3 Bd7 16. Qc3 $16 {[%cal Rg3g4,Rg2g4,Rh4h5]}) 13. Bh2 {[%cal Rg2g4]} f5 14. e3 O-O (14... Qxh4 15. Be2 O-O 16. Nf3 Qe7 17. O-O Qxb4 18. Rc1 $16)) 11. e3 Nbd7 12. Bd3 Nb6 13. Nf3 Bd7 14. O-O Nc4 (14... Qa8 $2 15. Bxc7 $18) (14... Ne4 15. Rc1 Nd6 16. Bxd6 cxd6 17. Qa7 Bc8 18. Bf1 h6 19. Nd2 $14 {[%cal Rd2b3,Rb3a5]}) 15. Qa7 Bxb5 (15... Qa8 16. Ra1 Qxa7 17. Rxa7 Bxb5 18. Rxb7 c6 19. Ra7 $16) 16. Qxb7 c6 (16... Nd6 17. Bxd6 Bxd3 18. Bxf8 Bxf1 19. Bxg7 Bxg2 20. Bxf6 Qxf6 21. Kxg2 $18) 17. Ra1 $14) (7... Nc6 {This ingenious (though rare) move must be taken seriously. Black aims to recapture on a5 with the knight after the smoke clears on the queenside.} 8. bxa5 axb5 9. axb5 Nxa5 10. e3 {The first step is always to open the diagonal for our bishop and buttress the b-pawn.} Bd7 11. Bg5 {An inventive alternative to the more conventional 11.Nf3, pinning the knight on f6 and inducing a major kingside weakening with ...h6-g5. If Black does not chase the bishop away, White will maintain lasting pressure on the kingside.} h6 (11... O-O 12. Nf3 h6 13. Bh4 $14 {Now that Black has castled kingside, ...g7-g5 comes with a far steeper price tag.} g5 14. Nxg5 $1 {In the finest traditions of the Jobava London! With the dark squared bishop gone, Black will have a very hard time insulating the kingside against the litany of threats that our bloodthirsty queen-bishop tandem will pose.} hxg5 15. Bxg5 Kg7 16. Qf3 {The queen is terribly overextended, paralyzing the knight on a5.} Qe7 17. Qg3 {Setting up the vicious threat of Bxf6+ and Qh4+, picking off Black's queen.} Rh8 18. Bd3 {Quiet and powerful. By completing our development, we ensure that our kingside attack will never fizzle out.} Rh5 19. h4 $18 {Black has nothing better than to give back the material with 19...Ne4.}) (11... Nc4 12. Bxc4 dxc4 13. Ne2 $1 {We readily sacrifice the pawn on b5 in order to complete our development. The idea is to properly time the trade on a8 and to follow up with Bxf6, permanently ruining Black's kingside pawn structure and offering White a lasting initiative.} Bxb5 14. e4 {Putting the bishop on g5 to work!} h6 (14... Rxa1 15. Qxa1 h6 16. Bxf6 {Black instantly pays the price for the sloppy capture on a1. The queen must babysit the a8-square, forcing Black to recapture with the pawn.} gxf6 (16... Qxf6 17. Qa8+ Qd8 18. Qxb7 $16) 17. O-O {White has fantastic compensation for the pawn, as defined by several factors: 1) The bishop on b5 has no active prospects, while the knight has countless ways to join the game, ranging from Nf4-Nh5 to the more creative d5 followed by Nd4. 2) Black's pawn structure on both sides of the board, but particularly the kingside, is horrific. 3) We control the center and the only open file on the board; 4) There is no shelter anywhere on the board for Black's king. The subsequent lines are obviously not forced, but they nicely illustrate the way in which these factors translate to a monster initiative.} Bc6 (17... O-O 18. Qc1 $1 Kh7 19. Qb1 $3 {Incredible geometry. After provoking ...Kh7 (which may seem counterproductive), the queen returns to b1, attacking the bishop and creating an x-ray against the King. But White does not follow up with e5+, which would be futile. The correct continuation is far more explosive!} Bc6 20. d5 $1 {There is the rub! The bishop is forced away from c6, leading to the destruction of Black's entire queenside.} Bd7 21. Qxb7 $1 $16 {What a variation! The zigzag pattern adopted by our queen leaves quite the lasting impression.}) 18. d5 {Smashing open the d-file, and carving out the d4-square for the knight.} exd5 19. Rd1 O-O 20. Qc1 Kh7 21. Ng3 $44 {[%csl Rb1,Rf5][%cal Rg3f5,Rc1b1] From a practical perspective, White's compensation for the two pawns is absolutely overwhelming. Black is facing a litany of threats in the center and on the kingside, the most immediate being Nf5.} Rg8 22. Qb1 {The queen shuttles back to b1, forcing Black's king to relinquish its control over the h6-pawn.} Kh8 23. Nf5 Qf8 24. Qc1 {Back to c1 we go! The pressure has reached a zenith, and in order to emerge unscathed, Black must uncork a series of jaw-dropping computer moves.} dxe4 25. Rd8 $1 {A lovely deflection sacrifice that would be decisive if not for an even crazier counter-combination.} Rxg2+ $3 {Luring the king to g2, upon which Black can intercept the c1-h6 diagonal with ...e3+ and pick up the rook on d8. Of course, we refuse to cooperate!} 26. Kf1 (26. Kxg2 e3+ $19 {[%cal Rc1h6,Rf8d8]}) 26... Qxd8 27. Qxh6+ Kg8 28. Kxg2 e3+ 29. Kg3 $40 {Even after finding all of these moves, Black still remains under serious pressure. 29...Qf8 will be met with 30.Qxf6, when Black must still demonstrate accuracy to hold the draw.}) 15. Rxa8 Qxa8 16. Bxf6 gxf6 17. O-O Qa5 18. d5 O-O 19. Qd2 Kh7 20. Qf4 $18) 12. Bh4 g5 {It is far from easy to make these moves, which permanently weaken Black's kingside. The resulting position - while objectively balanced - remains very difficult for Black to accurately navigate.} 13. Bg3 Ne4 (13... O-O 14. f3 {A typical prophylactic move, dealing a blow to the knight's prospects.} Nh5 15. Kf2 {Wonderfully inventive chess! The king embraces its new role as an integral part of the kingside mosaic. Its role on f2 will be revealed on the next move.} Kg7 (15... f5 {This weakens the e5 square and enables us to maintain the bishop pair.} 16. Be5 $16) 16. h4 $1 {And this, ladies and gentlemen, is the point of White's previous move! The h-pawn rushes forward to blast open the kingside, while the king - yes, the king - is assigned the role of keeping the bishop defended! Note that doing the same thing with the knight (15.Kf2) would have allowed the rather nasty possibility of ...Nc4. In almost all of these lines, the development of the knight is delayed until we have deployed our bishop to d3 in order to keep the a5-knight at bay.} Nxg3 17. Kxg3 b6 18. Bd3 {The king feels perfectly at home on g3, and the time has finally come to complete our development.} e5 19. dxe5 gxh4+ 20. Kf2 {Having done its job on g3, the indefatigable monarch ducks back to safety on f2.} Qg5 21. Ne2 $14 {While the position remains level, White's practical play is quite a bit easier. Our knight will find a lovely home on d4, while the rook on h1 keeps Black's queen tied down to the h4-pawn.}) 14. f3 $1 {Despite our outward lack of piece coordination, we are able to hold Black's knight at bay on the strenghth of some badass tactics.} Nxc3 $4 (14... Nd6 15. b6 $36) 15. Qd2 $18 {[%csl Ra5,Rc3][%cal Rd2a5] The knights are pinned, and Black loses a minor piece:} Nb3 16. Rxa8 Nxd2 17. Rxd8+ Kxd8 18. Kxd2 $18) (7... Nh5 {An ultra-rare and not particularly threatening move. We are more than happy to withdraw our bishop from f4.} 8. Bd2 axb5 (8... Bb6 9. e4 $1 {Using the compromised position of Black's knight to stake a claim in the center.} Nf6 (9... axb5 10. Qxh5 bxa4 11. e5 Nd7 12. Bg5 $1 $18) 10. e5 Ne4 11. a5 $1 {It turns out that Black's bishop is most certainly not out of the woods!} axb5 12. axb6 Rxa1 13. Qxa1 Nxd2 14. bxc7 Qxc7 15. Kxd2 O-O 16. Bd3 $14 {The smoke has finally cleared, and it is obvious that we are the one calling the shots. We can follow up by solidifying our central control with f2-f4. Black suffers from a lack of space and the permanent weakness of the b5-pawn.}) 9. axb5 c6 10. e3 {Underscoring the counterproductive nature of Black's seventh move. This move now comes with tempo, enabling us to recapture Black's bishop while keeping our queenside pawn structure fully intact.} Nf6 11. c4 $1 dxc4 12. Bxc4 $14 {Black's knight sally to h5 has been an abject failure: we are ready to recapture the bishop on a5, and Black has no way to recover the pawn.}) 8. axb5 $1 {It is crucial not to rush with taking the bishop, as it is pinned to the rook on a8.} Bd7 (8... Nh5 9. Bc1 $1 {An incredibly deep move. While 9.Bd2 is far more natural, the bishop is better placed in the following variation:} c6 10. e4 Nf6 11. e5 Ne4 12. Ne2 $16 {Had the bishop been on d2, Black could have captured it with their knight! As it stands, White is simply winning, since we will recapture the bishop and chase away or even trap the knight with f2-f3.}) (8... Bxb4 {This exchange sacrifice leads nowhere, since White can block the check on c3 by returning to d2.} 9. Rxa8 Bxc3+ 10. Bd2 Bxd2+ 11. Qxd2 {Clearly, Black has insufficient compensation for the exchange. Our next step is to calmly develop our kingside and ferry the king to safety.} Nbd7 12. e3 Nb6 (12... Ne4 13. Qb4 Nb6 14. Ra1 {Transposes to 12..Nb6 13.Ra1 Ne4 14.Qb4.}) 13. Ra1 Ne4 14. Qb4 Qe7 {An important moment. It is very tempting to agree to the queen trade, but the resulting position offers Black satisfactory compensation for the exchange due to his more active pieces and the long-term weakness of the b5-pawn.} 15. Qb1 {This is the best square for White's queen. On b1, it is far away from the predations of Black's knights, and will link up with bishop (after Bd3) to exert nagging pressure on the e4-knight. Note that ...Nc3 is nothing but an empty threat, in response to which we can simply bring our queen up to b3.} O-O 16. Nf3 Bd7 17. Bd3 $16 {We have successfully completed our kingsid development and kept Black's queenside counterplay at bay. We will follow up by castling and deploying the rook to c1. White's total control over all of the key queenside squares renders Black's compensation for the exchange virtually nonexistant.}) (8... c6 {A tricky move, linking up the queen and bishop and asking the b5-pawn a rather impolite question. It is crucial to avoid making concessions with 9.bxa5 or 9.bxc6, both of which play right into Black's hands by allowing him to stir the pot on the queenside. Instead, we keep the tension by reinforcing the b5-pawn.} 9. e3 Bd7 $1 {The only way to maintain a feasible position, although this move is not at all easy to find.} (9... cxb5 10. Bxb5+ Bd7 11. Bd3 {It is vital to keep the bishop pair.} Nc6 12. bxa5 Nxa5 13. Nf3 $14 {We have successfully navigated the queenside chaos, and it is clear that White will have all the fun in the middlegame. After castling, we can intensify the pressure with Ne5 and Bg5.}) (9... Ne4 {This move looks quite intimidating, but we are more than happy to part ways with the pawn on c3 in order to achieve rapid development and optimal piece coordination.} 10. bxa5 {This is the perfect opportunity to capture on a5, since Black cannot recapture due to the hanging knight on b8.} Nxc3 11. Qd2 Nxb5 12. Bd3 O-O 13. Nf3 $40 {The extra pawn is meaningless: our excellent piece coordination, bishop pair, and firm control over all key dark squares in the center grant us excellent prospects in the middlegame.}) (9... O-O $6 {This loses a pawn without a fight:} 10. Bxb8 Rxb8 11. Rxa5 $14) 10. b6 {We refuse to hand over control of the queenside! The lackluster 10.bxc6 would have played right into Black's hands by enabling the smooth development of the knight. This move, on the other hand, keeps Black's rook and knight frozen in place, and buys us a crucial tempo that we use to assert queenside dominance.} Qxb6 11. bxa5 {Obviously, a5 is untouchable since the knight hangs.} Qb2 (11... Rxa5 12. Bxb8 $18) 12. Ne2 {Cool, calm, and collected! All alone on our territory, the Black queen is fighting a lost cause.} O-O 13. Bd6 {This move underscores the importance of dark-square control in this variation. The bishop has a few questions to ask of the Black queen!} Re8 14. Ba3 Qb5 15. Nf4 Qxa5 16. Bb2 $18 {A lovely sequence indeed. Black loses an exchange for no compensation. Note the path taken by White's bishop, which started on f4 and ends all the way on the other side of the board!}) (8... b6 {With this move, Black reinforces the bishop on a5. He will have a passed pawn in the resulting position, but it will be far more of a weakness than a strength.} 9. bxa5 bxa5 (9... Rxa5 10. Rxa5 bxa5 11. Qa4 {With the rook gone, Black instantly loses the pawn.} Nbd7 12. e3 {Note that we do not even have to rush to take the pawn - it is going nowhere!} O-O 13. Bd3 Nb6 14. Qxa5 $16) 10. e3 O-O (10... Nbd7 11. Bd3 O-O 12. Nf3 Bb7 {see 10...0-0 11.Nf3 Nbd7 12.Bd3 Bb7.}) 11. Nf3 Nbd7 (11... c6 {This move almost never works tactically, since our bishop is trained at the knight on b8.} 12. Bxb8 Rxb8 13. bxc6 Rb6 14. Qa4 Qc7 15. Ne5 $16 {Black has no way to win back the pawn.}) 12. Bd3 Bb7 13. Ne5 {Oftentimes, this move is not as advisable with Black's knight on d7 and no bishop on d6, since Black can now simply capture our knight. In this specific position, however, we can effectively recapture on e5 with the pawn, driving away Black's knight and leading to an incredibly strong kingside attack.} Nxe5 14. dxe5 Nd7 (14... Ne4 15. Qh5 {The threat of f2-f3 renders Black's knight an utterly ineffective defender.} f5 16. exf6 Nxf6 (16... Qxf6 17. O-O Qxc3 18. Bxe4 dxe4 19. Rac1 {Picking up the pawn with interest.} Qf6 20. Rxc7 Rf7 21. b6 Rxc7 22. bxc7 $18) (16... gxf6 17. f3 $18 {[%csl Re4][%cal Rd3h7,Rh5h7]}) 17. Qe5 $16 {Black is dominated on the dark squares, and cannot defend e6 and c7 simultaneously.}) 15. Qh5 g6 16. Qh6 Qe7 (16... f6 17. Bxg6 $1) 17. h4 $16 {The march of the h-pawns spells big trouble for Black. Note that we can push h4-h5 against virtually any move, including ...f6. The attack is decisive.}) 9. e3 {[%cal Yf1b5] We still do not take the bishop!} Bxb4 {Now that White's bishop cannot return to d2 to block the check on c3, this is a far more serious idea. However, White's king is able to dodge the checks by journeying to f3!} (9... Ne4 {Hunting for the c-pawn is almost never effective, as Black loses a ton of tempi in the process and we are able to attain an ideal setup.} 10. bxa5 Nxc3 11. Qb3 Ne4 12. f3 Nd6 13. Bd3 $16) (9... b6 $6 10. bxa5 bxa5 11. Bd3 O-O 12. Qb1 $16 {Forming a queen-bishop battery, and preparing to meet 12...c6 with the devastating 13.b6.}) (9... c6 {Transposes to 8..c6 9.e3 Bd7.}) 10. Rxa8 $1 Bxc3+ 11. Ke2 Bxb5+ 12. Kf3 {Intrepid play is a prerequisite if you want to consider yourself a real Jobava player! As scary as this appears, Black simply does not have the firepower to exploit the king's compromised position. He has no choice but to spend time on castling and on activating his knight with ...Ne4; we will use this time to carve out shelter for our king on g2.} Bxf1 13. Qxf1 O-O (13... Ne4 14. Qb5+ $18) {Before building shelter for our king, we induce a queenside weakness and make it difficult for Black to untangle on the queenside.} 14. Qb5 {The best defense is attack! Despite appearances, White's king on f3 is perfectly safe, and we can hide it on g2 at our convenience.} b6 15. g3 {[%cal Rf3g2]} Ne4 16. h4 {...g5 must be stopped.} f6 17. Kg2 e5 {It appears that Black has trapped our bishop, since opening the f-file is unthinkable. But we have one more trick up our sleeve.} 18. f3 $1 {Counterattacking Black's knight, and forcing Black to relinquish his control over the c3-bishop. The tactics work out in White's favor.} Nd2 19. Ne2 {Another counterblow, this time hitting the bishop! If the bishop retreats to a5, we can finally save our bishop by taking twice on e5.} exf4 (19... Ba5 20. dxe5 fxe5 21. Bxe5 Nxf3 22. Bxg7 $1 Kxg7 23. Nf4 {Gorgeous tactics. The threat of Ne6+ forces Black to leave the knight on f3. Our king will journey back to the promised land to pick it up! This time, under the able protection of the knight, it will be quite a bit safer on that square. Note that 23...Qe7 should be met with 24.Qxd5, covering the e4-square.}) 20. Nxc3 fxe3 21. Re1 $18 {The smoke has cleared, and Black falls apart completely.}) 5. Nxd6+ {Thank you for the bishop pair!} cxd6 {This is one of the only lines where Black attains the pawn structure that we are used to having after Bd3 Bxd3 cxd3 (see typical idea #2). However, Black lacks a dark-squared bishop, which renders this pawn structure quite a bit less effective. The other bishop does not have active prospects, and preparing the ...e6-e5 pawn push - while opening up a diagonal for the bishop - will create even more targets in the center.} 6. e3 {White's advantage is defined by two factors: (1) the bishop pair and (2) the long-term pressure that we will exert against the d6-pawn.} Nc6 (6... Qb6 {This is best according to the computer, but it is virtually never played and does not really change the tenor of the position.} 7. Rb1 Qa5+ (7... O-O 8. c3 h6 9. h3 {It is quite important to throw this move in in response to ...h6, since Nf3 allows Black to pick up the bishop with ...Nh5.} Re8 10. Nf3 Ne4 11. Bh2 Bd7 12. Qb3 $14 {(Niemann) After the trade, Black will have a very hard time keeping d6 protected.}) 8. c3 Qxa2 9. Bxd6 $16 {Recovering the pawn and preventing Black from castling. Things are already quite bad for Black:} Ne4 10. Bc7 {A strange-looking move, but it prevents the queen from escaping via a5 and - much like Nb5 (see typical idea #7) - lures Black's knight to an incredible passive square.} Na6 11. Bf4 g5 12. Bg3 h5 13. Bd3 $1 $16 {Preparing to meet ...h4 with Be5 f6 Bxe4, when Black can only take one bishop at a time! White's advantage is not up for debate.}) (6... O-O {Black often starts with this move, but it will almost always transpose to the main line (6...Nc6) after Black develops the knight.} 7. c3 Nc6 (7... Re8 8. Nf3 Nc6 9. h3 {Transposes to 6...Nc6 7.Nf3 0-0 8.c3 Re8 9.h3.}) 8. Nf3 {Transposes to 6...Nc6 7.Nf3 0-0 8.c3.}) (6... e5 $2 {This is surprisingly common, but it simply drops a pawn for no compensation.} 7. dxe5 dxe5 8. Bxe5 Nc6 9. Bxf6 Qxf6 10. c3 O-O 11. Bd3 $16) 7. Nf3 O-O 8. c3 {It is always a good idea to buttress the d4-pawn, rendering ...e6-e5 even less effective.} Ne4 {Activating the knight and reinforcing the d6-pawn.} (8... Re8 9. h3 {Making luft for the bishop.} e5 {This move is the opposite of scary. The x-ray against White's king my appear concerning, but with the d4-pawn as secured as it is, Black has absolutely no way to convert his temporary development advantage into anything tangible. After we complete our kingside development, Black will experience long-lasting problems with his center.} 10. Bh2 Bf5 (10... exd4 11. Nxd4 Qb6 12. Qb3 Nxd4 13. Qxb6 Nc2+ 14. Kd2 axb6 15. Kxc2 $16 {Recovering the knight and leading to a miserable endgame for Black. His pawn structure says it all!}) (10... e4 11. Nd2 Bf5 {Under different circumstances, this type of pawn structure can favor Black, but with no dark-squared bishop, it makes a lot less sense. We have active ideas on both sides of the board.} 12. Be2 a6 13. Qb3 Qd7 14. g4 $1 {The pawn advances to g5, where it will either trap the knight or force the horsey to lose contact with the crucial d5-pawn.} Bg6 (14... Be6 15. g5 $18 {[%csl Rf6][%cal Re2h5] The knight is trapped!}) 15. g5 Nh5 16. Qxd5 $18) 11. Be2 {Since ...e4 is never scary, we simply continue our development.} Rc8 12. O-O h6 13. Qb3 {This is a great square for the queen, from where it pressures two pawns and stays out of the way of the rest of our pieces.} Qd7 14. Rfe1 {Continuing to improve our position by reinforcing the bishop, which will prove useful in the event that Black takes on d4.} exd4 (14... a6 15. a4 Bg6 16. Bf1 $14 {We can continue to improve with Rad1 and Qa2, paving the way for a potential pawn storm on the kingside. Black is saddled with tons of weaknesses in the center, and has no easy way to improve.}) (14... Bxh3 {This sacrifice is the opposite of scary.} 15. gxh3 Qxh3 16. Nd2 Ng4 17. Bxg4 Qxg4+ 18. Kh1 $16) 15. Nxd4 Nxd4 16. cxd4 $16 {It is also possible to take with the e-pawn, but we prefer this recapture, which opens up the queenside. This may seem to favor Black, but his control of the c-file is temporary - Black must burn a tempo to prevent Bb5, which we will use to contest the c-file with Rac1.}) (8... Qe7 9. Be2 e5 10. Bg3 Ne4 11. dxe5 $16 {Black drops the d-pawn.}) (8... h6 9. Be2 Re8 (9... Nh5 {This is not particularly scary. It is rather painful to part with our dark-squared bishop, but almost all of Black's problems will remain in the resulting position.} 10. O-O Nxf4 11. exf4 Qf6 12. Qd2 Bd7 13. Rfe1 Rfe8 14. Bf1 $14 {We have a nice space advantage and excellent prospects on the queenside.}) 10. h3 e5 11. Bh2 Bf5 12. O-O Ne4 13. Qb3 $14 {Hitting where it hurts! The only (tactical) way to defend both pawns is ...Qb6, but the queen trade further damages Black's pawn structure and makes it harder for them to defend d5.}) 9. Bd3 f5 10. h3 {This move recurs constantly in the 4...Bd6 line. In this case, we take the sting out of ...g7-g5.} Qf6 11. Bh2 {Tucking the bishop away and ensuring its permanent safety.} Bd7 12. O-O Be8 (12... e5 $2 {This move succumbs to a lovely tactical refutation that underscores how nicely our pieces are coordinated.} 13. dxe5 dxe5 14. Nxe5 $3 Nxe5 15. Bxe4 dxe4 16. Bxe5 $16 {Picking up the bishop on d7 and emerging a pawn ahead.}) 13. Ne1 $5 {[%cal Rf2f3] An inventive idea, preparing to kick away the knight with f2-f3. Black has no choice but to add another defender to d6.} Qe7 14. f3 Nf6 15. Bc2 {More excellent maneuvering. The bishop politely steps away to make room for the knight.} Bg6 {Preparing ...e5.} 16. Nd3 e5 17. Ba4 {The bishop has its moment in the sun! On a4, it hits the knight on c6, threatening to win a pawn and forcing Black to create serious dark-square weaknesses by advancing the pawn to e4.} e4 18. fxe4 dxe4 (18... fxe4 19. Nf4 Bf7 20. Qe1 $14 {The queen is ferried over to g3. We have the bishop pair, the superior pawn structure, and excellent prospects on the f-file.}) (18... Nxe4 19. Nf4 Bf7 20. Bb3 Nf6 21. Qf3 $16) 19. Nc1 $5 {An incredibly deep move, keeping contact with the f5-pawn so that Black's bishop cannot retreat to f7. White's control over the dark squares ensures total domination.} d5 20. Bxc6 bxc6 {It may seem that Black has fully rectified his pawn structure and has solved his positional problems, but they are in fact only beginning! Note that we have complete and permanent dominance over the dark-squares: the bishop on h2 is the hidden engine of our position. We can assert our advantage by activating our queen and knight, creating a situation in which Black is devoid of pawn breaks and of any active opportunities on either side of the board.} 21. Qa4 Qe6 22. Nb3 Nd7 23. Nc5 Nxc5 24. dxc5 $16 {The knight trade opens up the d4-square, which we can use as an outpost for our queen. Our bishop controls b8 and prevents Black from engineering the ...f4 pawn break. After we reposition our queen to b4, we can accumulate the queenside pressure with b2-b4 and a2-a4 (preparing b5), or blast open the center by preparing c3-c4. Even after an endless series of accurate moves, there is no end in sight to Black's suffering.} *

